---
globs:
  - "src/**/*.ts"
  - "test/**/*.ts"
  - "tsconfig*.json"
---

`tsconfig.base.json` compiles with `module` and `moduleResolution` set to `NodeNext`, so every relative import in `src/` and `test/` names the emitted file: `from "./x.js"`, `from "../settings/index.js"`. A bare `./x` or a `.ts` suffix can typecheck yet fail at runtime from `dist/`. Import the concrete module. The only intentional barrels are `src/settings/index.ts` (re-exporting `constants.ts`, `defaults.ts`, `featureModes.ts`, and `ENV` from `envKeys.ts`), `src/analytics/index.ts`, `src/prWorkspace/index.ts`, `src/agent/codemode/index.ts`, and `src/agent/execution/index.ts`. Do not add another `index.ts`, do not re-export a neighbour's symbol to shorten a path (`isLineResolutionPublishError` comes from `src/github/reviewErrors.js`, not `src/review/placement/`), and do not add `paths` aliases to any tsconfig. Use `import type` for type-only edges; `test/webImportGraph.test.ts` strips them when measuring the web runtime graph, so a value import of a Pi module widens the web bundle.
